{"name":"Pontoon","hex":"#0c608e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pontoon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flat-bottomed boat or other floating structure used as a buoyant support for a temporary bridge, dock or landing stage.","A bridge with floating supports."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A card game in which the object is to obtain cards whose value adds up to, or nearly to, 21 but not exceed it.","A two-card hand totalling 21 in this game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pontoon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Float (nautical)","blocks":[{"text":"A float is an airtight hollow structure, similar to a pressure vessel, designed to provide buoyancy in water. Its principal applications are in watercraft hulls, aircraft floats, floating piers, aquaculture, pontoon bridges, and marine engineering applications such as salvage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Supermarine_S.6B_ExCC.jpg/330px-Supermarine_S.6B_ExCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Float_(nautical)"},{"title":"Pontoon boat","blocks":[{"text":"A pontoon boat is a flattish boat that relies on floats to remain buoyant. These pontoons contain much reserve buoyancy and allow designers to create large deck plans fitted with a variety of accommodations including expansive lounge areas, stand-up bars, and sun pads. More horsepower is now able to be applied to the stern due to design improvements. Pontoon boat drafts may be as shallow as eight inches, which reduces risk of running aground and underwater damage; this allows it to come close to shore to pick up and drop off loads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Pontoon_boat.jpg/330px-Pontoon_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontoon_boat"},{"title":"Pontoon (card game)","blocks":[{"text":"Pontoon, formerly called vingt-un, is a card game of the banking family for three to ten players and the \"British domestic version of Twenty-One,\" a game first recorded in 17th-century Spain, but which spread to France, Germany and Britain in the late 18th century, and America during the early 19th century. It is neither a variant of nor derived from Blackjack. Both are descended from the early British version of vingt-un. In Britain, it first became known as pontoon during the First World War, the name apparently being a soldier's corruption of its former French name. The game has no official rules and varies widely from place to place. It is a popular family game, but also widely played by children, students, and members of the armed forces. In 1981, pontoon was the third most popular card game in Britain after rummy and whist. It has been described as \"an amusing round game and one which anyone can learn in a few minutes.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Pontoon-IMG_7475.jpg/330px-Pontoon-IMG_7475.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontoon_(card_game)"},{"title":"Pontoon (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Pontoon\" is a song recorded by American country music group Little Big Town. It was released in April 2012 as the first single from their fifth studio album, Tornado. The song, written by Natalie Hemby, Luke Laird and Barry Dean, became the group's first No. 1 single on the Billboard Hot Country Songs chart for the week of September 15, 2012. The song won the 2013 Grammy Award for Best Country Duo/Group Performance."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/36/PontoonLBT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontoon_(song)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Photo_of_pontoon_%28buoyant_platform%29_taken_at_Port_Vincent%2C_South_Australia.jpg/500px-Photo_of_pontoon_%28buoyant_platform%29_taken_at_Port_Vincent%2C_South_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Photo_of_pontoon_%28buoyant_platform%29_taken_at_Port_Vincent%2C_South_Australia.jpg/500px-Photo_of_pontoon_%28buoyant_platform%29_taken_at_Port_Vincent%2C_South_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An anchored platform supported by floats","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhoto_of_pontoon_(buoyant_platform)_taken_at_Port_Vincent%2C_South_Australia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/DeHavilland_Single_Otter_Harbour_Air.jpg/500px-DeHavilland_Single_Otter_Harbour_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/DeHavilland_Single_Otter_Harbour_Air.jpg/500px-DeHavilland_Single_Otter_Harbour_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Floats in use on a floatplane","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeHavilland_Single_Otter_Harbour_Air.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg/500px-2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg/1280px-2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg/500px-2019-03-16_01_Aquaculture_in_Chile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aquaculture fish farming in the fjords south of Castro, Chile","link":